# Atrax agent reference

You are working with Atrax, an agent-native CLI for small full-stack apps on Cloudflare Workers, Static Assets, and D1.

## Read the source of truth

Base URL: https://tarantula-9l0.pages.dev

1. Fetch `/llms.txt` for the documentation map.
2. Fetch `/docs.json` when you need a machine-readable page manifest.
3. Read the relevant Markdown page under `/docs/<slug>/index.md` before changing an app or running a remote operation.
4. Use `/llms-full.txt` only when the task crosses several product areas.

Do not treat planned features as available. The docs label every feature as available, mixed, or planned.

## Preserve the contract

- `atrax.json` is the app-owned, provider-neutral source of truth.
- `atrax.lock.json` stores stable, non-secret remote identities and should be committed.
- `.atrax/wrangler.jsonc` is generated. Never edit it.
- Migrations are ordered and immutable after application. Add the next migration instead of rewriting an applied one.

## Work safely

1. Read `atrax.json` and the relevant docs.
2. Run `atrax doctor --json` before deployment work.
3. Use `atrax plan --json` to see what a deploy would change. It never creates, migrates, or deploys anything.
4. Use `atrax deploy --dry-run` before a remote deploy when validating changes.
5. Use `atrax deploy --json` only when the user asked to deploy.
6. Verify remote state with `atrax inspect --json`.
7. Use `atrax drift --json` to check whether the provider still matches `atrax.lock.json`.
8. Use `atrax logs` for live request outcomes.

Exit codes: `0` success, `1` error (including a `plan` blocked by an unowned name conflict), `2` only from `drift` when the provider no longer matches the lockfile. Treat `2` as a state to report, not a crash.

The v0 chat template is public by default: anyone with its URL can read and post. Never describe it as private or suitable for secrets.

## Instant hosting (no Cloudflare account)

`atrax deploy` falls back to Atrax instant hosting when no Cloudflare account is detected, and `--instant` forces it. The app lands on a real public URL owned by Atrax's account. The claim token is printed once, by the deploy that mints it; it is never written to disk and redeploys never reprint it. Pass it to the user verbatim.

```sh
atrax deploy --instant --json
atrax claim <token> --json
```

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "status": "deployed",
  "mode": "instant",
  "name": "open-chat",
  "url": "https://i-3f9a2c81be.<subdomain>.workers.dev",
  "appId": "3f9a2c81be",
  "access": "public",
  "claimToken": "...",
  "expiresAt": 1790000000000,
  "resources": { "tables": { "name": "i-3f9a2c81be-tables" } }
}
```

```json
{ "schemaVersion": 1, "status": "claimed", "appId": "3f9a2c81be", "url": "https://i-3f9a2c81be.<subdomain>.workers.dev" }
```

`claimToken` and `expiresAt` (epoch milliseconds) are present only on the deploy that creates the app; later deploys reuse `.atrax/instant.json` and omit them. An unclaimed app is deleted 30 days after creation. Creates are limited to 10 per day per IP. `atrax.lock.json` records `"mode": "instant"`, and `inspect`, `logs`, `plan`, and `drift` refuse instant locks.

`access` is `"public"` or `"shared"` and reports who can open the URL. The human output says it in one line under the URL: a public app gets "This app is public: anyone with the URL can open it." Tell the user this verbatim when you report a public deploy; never imply the URL is private.

Instant hosting serves shared apps too. `"visibility": "shared"` deploys normally, instant hosting generates the app's Door session secret itself, and `atrax share` manages members through Atrax rather than a Cloudflare account. Use the same commands as below; the JSON is identical.

## Shared visibility (Door alpha slice)

`atrax.json` accepts `"visibility": "public"` (default) or `"visibility": "shared"`. A shared app is gated by the template's own Door module: deploy provisions a `DOOR_SESSION_SECRET` Worker secret once, and every request without a valid signed session cookie gets a 401 invitation page.

```sh
atrax share add ana@example.com --json   # returns a single-use invite URL
atrax share list --json                  # joined | invited | expired
atrax share remove ana@example.com --json
```

Atrax prints the invite URL. It does not send email; the owner shares the link. Members live in the app's own `door_members` table, whether the app runs in the caller's Cloudflare account or on instant hosting. This is an alpha slice, not the full Door product: no roles, teams, email delivery, or central session revocation. Do not describe a shared app as having sign-in, SSO, or roles.

## Common workflow

```sh
atrax new open-chat --template chat
cd open-chat
atrax dev
atrax plan --json
atrax deploy --json
atrax inspect --json
atrax drift --json
atrax share add ana@example.com --json
```

Return the stable app URL and important resource identities from JSON output. Keep provider noise out of user-facing summaries.
